<Project>

  <!--
    The meta package's own contribution, which is deliberately almost nothing: everything that acts
    arrives through the three dependencies. What is left is the one thing a meta package is uniquely
    able to say — where the starter files it carries are, on the machine that just restored them.

    ⚠ Once, quietly, and only when there is nothing to point at yet. `Rikarin.Skala.Canonical`'s
    target already reports a repository that has a `.editorconfig` without a canonical block; this
    one covers the case before that, where the file does not exist at all, and stays silent
    afterwards. Two packages shouting the same sentence is how a package gets removed.
  -->

  <!--
    ⚠ THE ADOPTION SWITCHES. docs/plan/02 § "Package boundaries" calls this package "the one-line
    adoption", and on the first repository to try it, it was the one line that could not be taken.

    `Rikarin.Skala.Sdk` brings `Rikarin.Skala.Rules`. On Vixen that is 16 SK3002 errors and 58
    further CS0246/CS0234 from the projects downstream of the ones that failed — not because SK3002
    ships at `error`, but because it ships at `warning` and real repositories set
    `TreatWarningsAsErrors`. Doc 09's whole mechanism for this is "accept the present, gate the
    future", and `.skala/baseline.sarif` is read by `skala check` and by nothing else, so that
    mechanism stops at the compiler's door: an analyzer package has no idea a baseline exists.

    ⚠ And the obvious escape hatch did not work. `ExcludeAssets="analyzers"` on a reference to this
    package is *silently* ineffective: NuGet's ExcludeAssets governs the assets of the package it is
    written on, and this package has none — the analyzer arrives from a transitive dependency whose
    .nuspec entry says include="All" (see the ProjectReference comment in the .csproj, where that
    attribute is load-bearing for the package working at all). So the consumer excludes nothing,
    reads no error, and is still handed /analyzer:Rikarin.Skala.Rules.dll.

    The two properties below are the actual switches, and they are honoured here rather than by
    NuGet, so they work regardless of how the dependency is declared.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      ⚠ Default true, and the opt-out is total: the analyzer is removed from the compiler's
      command line, not turned down. `false` is for a repository that wants the formatter and the
      canonical .editorconfig without the SK rules.
    -->
    <SkalaRulesEnabled Condition="'$(SkalaRulesEnabled)' == ''">true</SkalaRulesEnabled>

    <!--
      ⚠ Default FALSE, and this is the answer to the flag day.

      A repository that adopts Skala on Monday has a backlog it did not know about on Sunday, and
      `TreatWarningsAsErrors` turns that backlog into a tree that does not build. The three ways out
      of that are all bad: turn the rules off (then nothing is measured), turn the severities down
      in .editorconfig (docs/plan/09 § "no-new-suppressions" calls a severity turned down the
      widest suppression there is, and it is invisible in review), or spend a flag day fixing 16
      findings before anything else can happen.

      This is the fourth: the diagnostics fire, at their real severities, in the build log and in
      Rider — nothing is silenced and nothing is measured differently — they simply do not turn a
      warning into a build error until the repository says so. `skala check` on the `ci` gate against the
      committed baseline is what gates the future in the meantime, which is the mechanism doc 09
      actually designed. When the backlog is burned down, `<SkalaRulesAsErrors>true` is one line and
      one commit, and it is a decision somebody makes on purpose.

      ⚠ Scoped to Skala's own ids. It does not touch TreatWarningsAsErrors for anything else — a
      package that quietly made a repository's *other* warnings non-fatal would be doing the exact
      invisible thing this comment objects to.
    -->
    <SkalaRulesAsErrors Condition="'$(SkalaRulesAsErrors)' == ''">false</SkalaRulesAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    `$(SkalaRuleIds)` — every id `Rikarin.Skala.Rules` can emit, excluding SK0xxx (the formatter,
    which is not an analyzer) and SK9xxx (the tool talking about itself). It is a *list of ids*
    because csc's /warnaserror-: switch takes ids and has no prefix form; that is the only part of
    this that a person ever decided.

    ⚠ The value used to be typed here by hand, and it drifted on every rule merge — 36 ids against
    a rules.json shipping 101 at the worst of it, which is 65 rules turning a warning into a build
    error in every consumer with TreatWarningsAsErrors. It is now generated from
    Rules/Rikarin.Skala.Rules.Metadata/rules.json by the SkalaGenerateRuleIds target in this
    package's .csproj, on every build, into the file imported below. rules.json is already the
    single source for the DiagnosticDescriptors, the docs/rules/ pages, `skala explain`, the SARIF
    rules[] block and the ReSharper severity mapping; this was the one consumer with the generation
    step missing.

    ⚠ The import is unconditional on purpose. A missing file is MSB4019 on the first build of the
    first repository that references the package — loud — whereas Condition="Exists(...)" would
    make an empty $(SkalaRuleIds) silently disable the whole escape hatch below, which is the
    failure this file exists to prevent.
  -->
  <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Rikarin.Skala.Sdk.RuleIds.props" />

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(SkalaRulesAsErrors)' != 'true' and '$(TreatWarningsAsErrors)' == 'true'">
    <WarningsNotAsErrors>$(WarningsNotAsErrors);$(SkalaRuleIds)</WarningsNotAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    ⚠ Removing the item is the only opt-out that works. The analyzer reaches csc as an @(Analyzer)
    item resolved from the assets file, and nothing a consumer can write on the PackageReference
    takes it back out again (see above). Matched on the assembly name so that a repository
    referencing `Rikarin.Skala.Rules` directly, for its own reasons, is unaffected by this package's
    switch — this removes what this package brought.
  -->
  <Target Name="SkalaRemoveRules" BeforeTargets="CoreCompile" Condition="'$(SkalaRulesEnabled)' == 'false'">
    <ItemGroup>
      <Analyzer Remove="@(Analyzer)" Condition="'%(Filename)' == 'Rikarin.Skala.Rules'" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <SkalaSdkAdoptionNotice Condition="'$(SkalaSdkAdoptionNotice)' == ''">true</SkalaSdkAdoptionNotice>
    <SkalaSdkStarterDirectory Condition="'$(SkalaSdkStarterDirectory)' == ''">$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../content</SkalaSdkStarterDirectory>
    <SkalaSdkEditorConfig Condition="'$(SkalaSdkEditorConfig)' == ''">$([MSBuild]::GetPathOfFileAbove('.editorconfig', '$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)'))</SkalaSdkEditorConfig>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <Target Name="SkalaSdkAdoption"
          BeforeTargets="CoreCompile"
          Condition="'$(SkalaSdkAdoptionNotice)' == 'true' and '$(SkalaSdkEditorConfig)' == ''">

    <Message Importance="high"
             Text="Skala: no .editorconfig at or above $(MSBuildProjectDirectory). Start from $([System.IO.Path]::GetFullPath('$(SkalaSdkStarterDirectory)/skala.editorconfig')) and $([System.IO.Path]::GetFullPath('$(SkalaSdkStarterDirectory)/skala.jsonc')), then run `skala config sync --apply`." />
  </Target>

</Project>
